.TH SEGATTACH 2
.SH NAME
segattach, segalloc, segbrk, segdetach \- map or allocate memory segments
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "segattach :: proc(fd: int) -> (addr: uintptr, err: i64)"
.B "segalloc :: proc(bytes: int, flags: u64 = 0) -> (addr: uintptr, err: i64)"
.B "segbrk :: proc(addr: uintptr, top: uintptr) -> i64"
.B "segdetach :: proc(addr: uintptr) -> i64"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Segattach
maps the device behind the open descriptor
.I fd
into the caller's address space, and answers the address it landed at.
The descriptor must name a device the kernel can hand out as memory,
.B /dev/fb
being the one in the tree today; a stream, which is almost every other
file, answers
.BR ENODEV .
The mapping is writable and never executable, and lasts as long as the
descriptor's holder does.
.PP
.I Segalloc
asks the kernel for
.I bytes
of anonymous memory, rounded up to whole pages, and answers the address
it landed at.
This is how a program holds more than its own image: static data is all
a compiled program's format gives it, and a window's pixels or a
program's heap come from here instead.
The memory is zero, writable and never executable.
.I Flags
is zero for a run that is private to the process \(em shared under
.B RFMEM
and copied otherwise, like a data segment (see
.IR fork (2))
\(em or
.B abi.SEGSHARED
for a run that every fork shares regardless of its flags and every
.I exec
keeps (see
.IR exec (2)).
A request of zero bytes, or of more than the kernel's bound on one run,
answers
.BR EINVAL ;
no room left, on the machine or in the process's own table of segments,
answers
.BR ENOMEM .
.PP
.I Segbrk
moves the top of a run the caller already holds from
.I segalloc .
.I Addr
is any address inside the run, and
.I top
is where it should end, rounded up to a whole page; a
.I top
of zero answers the run's base without moving anything.
The run's own address never moves: growing keeps what was there and
zero-fills the new pages, and shrinking gives the tail back.
Only a run from
.I segalloc
may be resized this way; naming any other kind of segment answers
.BR EINVAL .
A run shared under
.B SEGSHARED
may grow but may not shrink while more than one process holds it.
.PP
.I Segdetach
removes the segment covering
.I addr
from the caller's address space; its memory is freed once the last
holder has detached it.
A process may detach only what it attached or allocated, never the text,
data or stack it was given at birth; naming an address outside any
segment, or inside one of those three, answers
.BR EINVAL .
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/sys.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/syscall.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (2),
.IR fork (2),
.IR exec (2),
.IR rendezvous (2),
.IR fb (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Segattach
and
.I segalloc
answer the address and a zero error on success, or a zero address and a
negative
.B \-errno
on failure.
.I Segbrk
and
.I segdetach
answer zero or a negative
.BR \-errno .
See
.IR errstr (2).
.SH BUGS
A process may hold at most forty segments, and one run of anonymous
memory may not exceed four megabytes.
